# Pagani region overlay + per-region identity for the unified pagani build.
#
# Project IDs (from ro.boot.prjname, set by the bootloader):
#   24875  OnePlus 13s   (CPH2723, India)  OP612BL1
#   24821  OnePlus 13T   (PKX110,  China)  OP60F5L1
#
# The overlay service runs once early-boot, before any HAL service that
# reads /odm files. The identity property triggers fire whenever
# ro.boot.prjname is set (always set by bootloader).

# Region overlay is performed by init.pagani-region-binds.rc (auto-generated
# alongside the CN /odm payload by tools/build_pagani_cn_vendor.sh). Each
# diff-content file gets one mount-bind line under the prjname=24821
# property trigger, executed by init in init context. No daemon or custom
# sepolicy domain needed.

# Per-region runtime markers. Note: ro.product.* are set in first-stage
# init from build.prop and cannot be changed here, so the build identity
# stays as the IN/CPH2723 default on both regions. That's cosmetic only;
# OEM HALs and apps key off ro.boot.prjname and the marker below, not
# ro.product.model.
on property:ro.boot.prjname=24875
    setprop persist.sys.oplus.region IN

on property:ro.boot.prjname=24821
    setprop persist.sys.oplus.region CN

# HAL service gating. The OEM ships its own init.rc files alongside the
# eSIM (IN) and eID (CN) HAL service binaries (extracted to
# /odm/etc/init/esim@1.0-service.rc etc). Those .rc files declare the
# HIDL interfaces correctly. We don't redeclare them here to avoid
# host_init_verifier failures on unknown HIDL interfaces.
#
# Region gating: each OEM init.rc starts its service on `class hal`
# trigger which fires for every region. To prevent the wrong-region
# service from starting we stop it explicitly when prjname doesn't
# match. The service binary is harmless if started — it just exits
# because the region's hardware path isn't present.
on property:ro.boot.prjname=24875
    stop vendor.oplus.hardware.eid@1.0-service

on property:ro.boot.prjname=24821
    stop vendor.oplus.hardware.esim@1.0-service
